<h2 id="launch-plan">How to start a side hustle in San Jose this month</h2>
<ol class="steps">
  <li><strong>Check your employment agreement.</strong> If you work in tech, confirm what your employer allows before you take paid side work.</li>
  <li><strong>Choose one offer and one price.</strong> Use the <a href="/tools/freelance-rate-calculator/">freelance rate calculator</a> to set a rate that covers taxes, software and downtime.</li>
  <li><strong>Register your business.</strong> File for a San Jose Business Tax Certificate within 90 days of starting. Santa Clara, Sunnyvale and other nearby cities have their own rules.</li>
  <li><strong>Find your first three clients through your network.</strong> Former coworkers, parent groups, Nextdoor and alumni Slack channels convert far better than cold platforms.</li>
  <li><strong>Collect testimonials.</strong> Ask every early client for a review or LinkedIn recommendation.</li>
  <li><strong>Track every dollar.</strong> Use a separate account and set aside taxes from every payment.</li>
</ol>

<h2 id="tax-rules">California and San Jose tax rules for side income</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>State income tax:</strong> California rates run from 1% to 12.3%, plus 1% more on income above $1 million. Side income stacks on top of your salary, so a tech worker's freelance profit can land in a high bracket. Pay quarterly estimates to the Franchise Tax Board.</li>
  <li><strong>Self-employment tax:</strong> 15.3% federal on net profit (the Social Security portion stops at the annual wage base, which matters if your salary is already high). Our <a href="/tools/self-employment-tax-calculator/">self-employment tax calculator</a> helps estimate it.</li>
  <li><strong>AB5:</strong> California's ABC test means companies often prefer to hire freelancers who clearly run independent businesses. Keep multiple clients, your own equipment and your own schedule.</li>
  <li><strong>San Jose Business Tax Certificate:</strong> Required for businesses operating in the city, including self-employed people, independent contractors and home-based or part-time businesses. The base tax is adjusted each year, and very low-revenue businesses can request an exemption. The certificate is not a zoning or permit approval.</li>
  <li><strong>Sales tax:</strong> Selling physical goods requires a seller's permit from the California Department of Tax and Fee Administration.</li>
</ul>
<div class="callout callout-warn"><strong>Watch out:</strong> If your salary withholding is set for a W-2 job only, side profit can leave you owing a large bill in April. Raise your withholding or pay quarterly estimates as soon as side income becomes regular.</div>

